      <span class="tg-hero__type">Part V of Five</span>
      <h1 class="tg-hero__title">Spiritual Abuse: Korah, Jeremiah, and the Use of a Text as a Muzzle</h1>
      <p class="tg-hero__intro">
        Somewhere this week, someone raised a concern about a leader and was told they had
        "a spirit of Korah." That sentence is this passage's most frequent real-world
        application, and it is almost always a misuse. But "almost always" is not "always,"
        and a treatment that simply inverts the polemic — making every leader Eli and every
        critic Jeremiah — has only changed which side gets to skip the argument.
      </p>
      <blockquote class="tg-hero__key-verse">
        <p>Woe to the shepherds of Israel who have been feeding themselves! … With force and
           harshness you have ruled them.</p>
        <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Ezekiel 34:2">Ezekiel 34:2</a>, 4</cite>
      </blockquote>
    </div>
  </header><main>
